    <owl:Ontology rdf:about="http://kbpedia.org/kko/rc/">
        <owl:versionIRI rdf:resource="http://kbpedia.org/rc/v300"/>
        <owl:imports rdf:resource="file://C:/1-ActivePython/kbpedia/working/build/stubs/kko.owl"/>
        <owl:imports rdf:resource="https://www.w3.org/2009/08/skos-reference/skos-owl1-dl.rdf"/>
        <ns:license rdf:resource="http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"/>
        <kko:prefURI>http://www.kbpedia.org/ontologies/kbpedia_reference_concepts.owl</kko:prefURI>
        <dc:creator xml:lang="en">Michael K. Bergman</dc:creator>
        <dc:date xml:lang="en">TO BE UPDATED PRIOR TO RELEASE.</dc:date>
        <dc:dateCopyrighted rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2016-06-01T20:00:00Z</dc:dateCopyrighted>
        <dc:description xml:lang="en">KBpedia is a foundational ontology for capturing the essential components (or &apos;primitives&apos;) of knowledge bases. It is an amalgam of six of the largest public knowledge bases available -- Wikipedia, DBpedia, Wikidata, GeoNames, schema.org, and the UN Standard Products and Services Code (UNSPSC). It is designed for mapping and interoperating diverse ontologies to one another. The ultimate objective is to provide an ontology that surfaces and organizes the features within knowledge bases most useful to machine learning, artificial intelligence, data interoperability, schema mapping, and fact tagging and extraction.

The topl-level structure of KBpedia is governed by the KBpedia Knowledge Ontology. KKO is informed by the semiotics (theory of signs) articulated by the 19th century American logician, Charles Sanders Peirce. The design is based on the triadic ideas of Firstness, Secondness and Thirdness that informed virtually all of Peirce&apos;s formulations in logic and philosophy. 

Peirce&apos;s theory of signs reflects a continual process of the understanding of what a sign means evolving through truth-testing and community use and consensus. The ultimate basis of a sign resides in the idea or quality of something (a Firstness), which has a monadic relationship to itself; an object which is described or made evident in relation to these qualities through dyadic relationships (a Secondness); and then which is understood or perceived by an interpretant (a Thirdness), such as an intelligent human being. (Though in broadest terms, semiosis is not limited to human mediation, and may apply to other agents, such as artificial intelligence.) All signs embody all three of these triadic aspects.

Signs build upon signs -- such is the case with human languages and the concepts conveyed by them -- and ultimately are subject to logic and questioning, the results of which may cause the nature of the relationships between these aspects of signs to undergo revision and refinement. Semiosis is thus intimately related to logic and is itself a continuous and community-oriented process.

Words and language are themselves a Thirdness, which is the result of signmaking consensus. The semiotic or signmaking process builds from things as they are or may be to an ability to name and describe them though language -- and then ultimately to reason and argue over them leading to shared understandings, laws and complex notions of human concepts and worldviews. Each successive progression of signs involves this interplay of requisite Firstness, Secondness and Thirdness. This triadic nature of signs provides a coherent way to understand and organize the representation of knowledge at any level.

Some 60,000 concepts are built upon the KKO to product the full KBpedia. KBpedia has a modular design built from typologies, which are major branches within this baseline knowledge base.</dc:description>
        <dc:publisher xml:lang="en">Semantics Press LLC</dc:publisher>
        <vann:preferredNamespacePrefix>kbpedia</vann:preferredNamespacePrefix>
        <vann:preferredNamespaceUri>http://kbpedia.org/kko/rc/</vann:preferredNamespaceUri>
        <owl:priorVersion xml:lang="en">Version 1.00 of the KBpedia Knowledge Ontology (KKO), released Aug 6, 2016, was the first commercial release of the system. It was a rather complete re-write and re-organization of the UMBEL (Upper Mapping and Binding Exchange Layer) ontology, significantly modified to accommodate its purpose for integrating knowledge bases (KBs).</owl:priorVersion>
        <owl:priorVersion xml:lang="en">Version 1.10 of KKO and KBpedia, released Nov 15, 2016, focused on two major updates. First, certain aspects of the upper structure of KKO were streamlined. And, second, KBpedia’s core typologies, which capture the overwhelming majority of reference concepts that are classified as entity types, were further organized to create tighter taxonomic structures.</owl:priorVersion>
        <owl:priorVersion xml:lang="en">Version 1.20 of the KBpedia Knowledge Ontology (KKO) and KBpedia, released Dec 5, 2016, was a fairly significant re-write of the KKO upper structure. This update was driven by two factors: 1) a revisiting of some assignments due to the attention given actions and predicates (specifically, Actions, Events, Situations, Relations) as opposed to earlier efforts that focused on Entities and Classes (Generals); and 2) an attempt to both rely more directly on Peircean terminology where it made sense and to rely less directly on that terminology where it was obscure or dated.</owl:priorVersion>
        <owl:priorVersion xml:lang="en">Version 1.30 of KKO, released on Jan 27, 2017, was an internal release version only. This version tested procedures for reverse mapping of sub-graph structures within a source knowledge base (B) to those of the target KBpedia knowledge structure (A). Most conventional mappings are of the B --&gt; A form, where what is mapped from B requires a direct tie-in point in A. Reverse mapping, tentatively called reciprocal mapping, identifies and places new structure from B into A, resulting in the target now being (B+A).</owl:priorVersion>
        <owl:priorVersion xml:lang="en">Version 1.40 of the KBpedia Knowledge Ontology (KKO) and KBpedia, released Feb 28, 2017, was the result of reciprocal mapping from Wikipedia to KBpedia. Reciprocal mapping adds sub-graph structure and new nodes to the target knowledge base. Nearly 40% more reference concepts were added to KBpedia in this v 1.40 release compared to prior versions of KBpedia.</owl:priorVersion>
        <owl:priorVersion xml:lang="en">Version 1.50 of KKO and KBpedia, released Sep 13, 2017, marked a major update. KKO properties were updated and organized according the three main property splits into Attributes, (external) Relations, and Representations (or Annotations) according to Peircean principles and the universal categories of Firstness, Secondness and Thirdness. Version 1.50 also used this structure to introduce a comprehensive mapping against Wikidata properties.</owl:priorVersion>
        <owl:priorVersion xml:lang="en">Version 1.60 of KKO and KBpedia, released Oct 1, 2018, marked the first open-source version of KBpedia. It greatly expanded mappings to Wikidata and Wikipedia and moved to address gaps in the definitions of resource concepts (RCs). However, because of time pressures, not all open-source updates were completed for this release.</owl:priorVersion>
        <owl:priorVersion xml:lang="en">Version 2.00 of KBpedia and KKO, released Jan 23, 2019, provided full mappings to Wikidata and Wikipedia, as well as complete definitions for all RCs (reference concepts). Duplicates were checked and many minor typology corrections were made. This version was the first, complete, open-source release of KBpedia.</owl:priorVersion>
        <owl:priorVersion xml:lang="en">Version 2.00 of KBpedia and KKO, released Jan 23, 2019, provided full mappings to Wikidata and Wikipedia, as well as complete definitions for all RCs (reference concepts). Duplicates were checked and many minor typology corrections were made. This version was the first, complete, open-source release of KBpedia. Because of these substantial changes, the version numbers were skipped forward.</owl:priorVersion>
        <owl:priorVersion xml:lang="en">Version 2.10, released Mar 27, 2019, resulted in instance mappings for Wikidata achieving 99% coverage. All 45 K mappings were manually checked, resulting in a further 1.25% correction of assignments. About half of existing mappings to Wikidata categories were converted to be represented by the topic entity. Wikipedia mappings have been similarly improved.</owl:priorVersion>
        <owl:priorVersion xml:lang="en">Version 2.21 of KKO and KBpedia was updated Dec 7, 2019, with minor placement improvements from the original v 2.20, released Nov 11, 2019. This version was devoted to clean up of the individual typologies in the system, the removal of long species lists and individuals, some re-organization of the results, and removal of UMBEL linkages due to the retirement of that system. Nearly 2000 reference concepts (RCs) were removed from KBpedia due to these changes.</owl:priorVersion>
        <owl:priorVersion xml:lang="en">Version 2.50 of KKO and KBpedia, released Feb 10, 2020, was a major upgrade that resulted in version numbers skipping forward. The major change in this version was to completely integrate the UN Standard Products and Services Codes (UNSPSC) as the seventh core knowledge base included in the system, joining Wikidata, Wikipedia, OpenCyc, schema.org, GeoNames, and DBpedia. This addition brought ecommerce and marketing capabilities to KBpedia. The Products typology was split into three parts and better disjointedness tests were added to the system. 

The addition of UNSPSC added a net of about 5000 reference concepts (RCs) to the system, including identification and removal of about 700 duplicate RCs from the merge. (Total RCs exceed 58 K.) Mappings to Wikidata and Wikipedia were also made for the UNSPSC structure, which contains all three levels of the four-level UNSPSC structure, as well as thousands of the &apos;commodity&apos; level items in it. 

Property mappings to UNSPSC, Wikidata, Wikipedia, and GeoNames, while previously available in separate mapping files, were also added to the base KBpedia system. Note that these KBpedia mappings are still available as separate files.</owl:priorVersion>
        <owl:versionInfo xml:lang="en">Version 3.0 is the first of KBpedia to be built with the cowpoke package. Major changes that have occurred with this release are: 1) dropping of the mappings to OpenCyc, since that system has been abandoned by its developers, Cycorp; 2) replacement of all hyphens in IRI names with underscores to make the system more friendly to Python development; 3) a winnowing of the typology assignments to reduce non-germane ones (about two-thirds of prior assignments have been eliminated without affecting the intent or disjointedness assertions of prior versions; and 4) general bug fixes and updated of values and mapped items in the five years since the prior release.</owl:versionInfo>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">KBpedia</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The KBpedia full structure is, one, a vocabulary of classes and predicates for describing domain ontologies, and, two, a logical knowledge graph for reasoning and extending for domain purposes, with the specific aim of promoting interoperability with external datasets and domains.</skos:definition>
        <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">In KBpedia, &apos;CSP&apos; is a shorthand for Charles Sanders Peirce (see https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_Sanders_Peirce), whose writings have had a great influence on the design of this knowledge graph. 

Also, some CSP quotes are derived from the following sources:

CP x.y =Collected Papers of Charles Sanders Peirce, volume x, paragraph y. 

See https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_Sanders_Peirce_bibliography.</skos:editorialNote>
        <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">To our knowledge, KBpedia is the first knowledge representation usable by computers that attempts to embrace Peircean principles in all of its aspects. Being first also means little scrutinized. We hope other scholars of Charles Sanders Peirce (CSP, pronounced &quot;purse&quot;) contribute with their understandings.

The history of the KBpedia also shows periodic, big chunks of change. The ideas behind KBpedia are grand and encompassing. The writings of CSP are subtle and profound. Further, CSP&apos;s writings are still being transcribed and organized, and it is difficult to get what does exist in the literature in electronic form. The net result is sometimes new understandings and change in the KBpedia structure.

Any attempt to capture the ideas of Charles Sanders Peirce or to be guided by an understanding of his teachings is fraught with many difficulties. His views shifted over the years; he was not always in a position to completely explicate his views; and much of his writing still remains untranscribed. Such is the nature of facts trying to lead to truth.

We welcome input at any time about how best to capture Peircean ideas into a computable knowledge graph. Useful input will be readily acknowledged, including in future updates of the KBpedia Knowledge Ontolgy (KKO) or the full KBpedia.

Peircean scholars have been the first source of information here. A good starting point is the entire Peirce category on Wikipedia (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Charles_Sanders_Peirce). There are speciality Peircean online sources too numerous to list that are also useful from these starting links.

Another resource is the Peirce online discussion list: http://www.iupui.edu/~arisbe/PEIRCE-L/PEIRCE-L.HTM.</skos:editorialNote>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">KBpedia - Full Structure</skos:prefLabel>
    </owl:Ontology>
    


